{
  "teamId": 108,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Los Angeles Angels were a team that never quite found its footing, stumbling out of the gate in April and never fully recovering. From the opening weeks, the pitching staff hemorrhaged runs at an alarming rate, with Jordan Romano, Drew Pomeranz, and Joey Lucchesi all surrendering pivotal home runs that set the tone for a long, painful summer. A team that scored 655 runs while allowing 752 was simply not built to compete, and the early months made that abundantly clear.\n\nThere were genuine moments of hope scattered throughout the summer. Zach Neto emerged as a legitimate offensive force, launching clutch home runs in May and June that briefly ignited the fanbase. Vaughn Grissom's grand slam on May 26th — with Mike Trout, Zach Neto, and Logan O'Hoppe all crossing the plate — was the kind of play that made Angels fans believe. A remarkable June 24th rally, fueled by singles from Wade Meckler, Nolan Schanuel, and Logan O'Hoppe, showed flashes of what this lineup could be when everything clicked together.\n\nBut the bullpen remained the team's achilles heel throughout. Kirby Yates surrendered critical home runs to Freddie Freeman and Jeff McNeil. Ben Joyce imploded in late August against Texas. Sammy Peralta gave up a devastating grand slam to Edmundo Sosa on August 28th, one of the most damaging single plays of the entire season. Every time the Angels clawed back into relevance, the bullpen found a way to extinguish the fire.\n\nThe final months were a dispiriting march toward a 62-100 finish. Mike Trout, still producing late in September, offered a reminder of what this franchise once was and could be again. Young players like Denzer Guzman and Vaughn Grissom flashed enough potential to provide cautious optimism for the future. But 2026 belonged to the long tradition of Angels disappointment — a season defined by blown leads, battered pitching, and the quiet sorrow of fading October relevance long before the leaves began to fall.",
